# Developer Portfolio Website – Java & Spring Boot

## 📌 Project Overview
This project is a **fully functional and responsive developer portfolio website**
built using **Java, Spring Boot, Thymeleaf, and Bootstrap 5**.

The portfolio is designed to showcase technical skills, real-world projects,
and GitHub work in a **recruiter-friendly and professional manner**.

The project strictly follows the **Software Development Life Cycle (SDLC)**,
simulating real-world engineering practices.

---

## 🎯 Project Objectives
- Build a professional portfolio for Java developers
- Clearly showcase skills, projects, and technical expertise
- Provide easy access to GitHub, resume, and contact details
- Apply SDLC concepts in a real-world project
- Create a clean, responsive, and content-focused UI

---

## 👥 Target Audience
- Recruiters
- Hiring managers
- Technical interviewers
- Java developers and students

---

## 🧩 Planned Features
- Home / About Me section
- Skills section (technical stack)
- Projects section with GitHub links
- Resume download
- Contact form
- Responsive UI using Bootstrap 5

---

## 🛠 Tech Stack
### Backend
- Java
- Spring Boot
- Spring MVC

### Frontend
- Thymeleaf
- HTML5
- CSS3
- Bootstrap 5

### Database
- MySQL (for contact form messages)

### Tools
- Git & GitHub
- IntelliJ IDEA
- Maven

---

## 🏗 System Architecture
The project follows the *MVC (Model–View–Controller)* architecture:

- *Controller*: Handles HTTP requests
- *Service*: Business logic
- *Repository*: Data access layer
- *View*: Thymeleaf templates

---

## 🔁 SDLC-Based Development Plan (15 Days)

### Phase 1: Requirement Analysis
- Research real-world portfolios
- Identify pain points
- Define functional & non-functional requirements

### Phase 2: Planning
- Finalize tech stack
- Create 15-day roadmap
- Decide deployment strategy

### Phase 3: System Design
- High-Level Design (Architecture)
- Low-Level Design (Entities, controllers)

### Phase 4: Development
- Spring Boot project setup
- UI design with Bootstrap
- Backend integration using Thymeleaf
- Implement portfolio modules

### Phase 5: Testing
- Functional testing
- UI & responsiveness testing

### Phase 6: Deployment
- Deploy application to production server

### Phase 7: Maintenance
- Bug fixes
- UI improvements
- Content updates

